```cpp
double dval; int ival; int *pi;
dval = ival = pi = 0;
```

Because the variables' types do not match or are not implicitly convertible to one another. In particular, ival = p = 0 tries to assign a pointer value (address 0) to an int.

Correction:

```cpp
double dval; int ival; int *pi;
dval = ival = 0; // Note the implicit conversion.
pi = nullptr;
```

